Visual Pin Layout Key
ESP32-CAM Board Section
GND Pin: Power ground input from the Buck converter, the diagonal leg of the shutter button, and the charging BMS output (common ground).
5V Pin: Main 5V power input (must be adjusted to exactly 5.0V) coming from the Buck Converter OUT+.
3.3V Pin: Used only to connect the first leg of the 10kΩ resistor to create the logical Pull-Up signal.
GPIO 13 Pin: Connected to both the logical signal line (the junction of the resistor and the button leg). When the button closes diagonally across GND, this pin drops to logical LOW.
4-Pin Shutter Button Section
Because 4-pin tactile switches can be confusing, the diagonal rule is the only foolproof method.
Place the 4-pin rectangular switch so it straddles the breadboard center gap.
Wire Top-Left leg to ESP32-CAM GND.
Wire Bottom-Right leg (diagonal leg) to ESP32-CAM GPIO 13 and the 10kΩ resistor.
